languages—even widely spoken ones—struggle to match this standard. Current practices of collecting and filtering web data from the Internet lag behind English performance due to the Internet’s inherent English-centric nature (Bender et al., 2021; Imani et al., 2023).
One direction to address the data quality issue is using pretrained language models to generate highquality synthetic data (Maini et al., 2024; Abdin et al., 2024). However, language model-based data generation is not suitable for multilingual research due to limited language coverage. For example, one of the current strongest models with multilingual support, Llama 3.2 (Dubey et al., 2024), officially supports fewer than 20 languages. For lowresource languages like Welsh, the near-absence of well-supported large language models makes data generation practically impossible. Furthermore, even with proprietary language models such as GPT-4, which might have better coverage, generation at the trillion-token scale is non-trivial.1
To address the limited language coverage issue, machine translation (MT) offers a potential solution for creating multilingual datasets using taskspecific neural machine translation (NMT) models, with the goal of preserving contextual, idiomatic, and pragmatic nuances across languages. However, this line of research (Urbizu et al., 2023; Doshi et al., 2024) remains underexplored for pretraining.
In this work, we introduce TransWebEdu, a large-scale multilingual corpus created by translating a subset of FineWeb-edu (Lozhkov et al., 2024), a high-quality English dataset, into nine diverse languages using NLLB-200-1.3B (Costajussà et al., 2022). The resulting corpus spans ten languages—Arabic, French, German, Indonesian, Italian, Russian, Spanish, Swahili, Welsh, and English—exceeding 100B tokens per language, with a total of 1.7 trillion tokens. We evaluate the ef
