 Translation Model Although various models support translation tasks, including neural machine translation (NMT) models and LLMs (Stahlberg, 2020; Alves et al., 2024; Martins et al., 2024). However, exploration of LLMs’ translation performance for medium- and low-resource languages remains limited. For example, TowerLLM (Alves et al., 2024), an open multilingual LLM designed for translation-related tasks, supports only 10 languages. In contrast, NMT models are generally more accessible and widely adopted for translation, particularly for low-resource languages, due to more than a decade of dedicated development in this field (Stahlberg, 2020). A prominent example is NLLB-200 (Costa-jussà et al., 2022), an open-source suit of models capable of providing high-quality translations for 200 languages. These models are purpose-built to support linguistic diversity and are optimized for robust performance, even for resource-scarce languages.
In our study, we assess whether constructing documents using sentence-level translations can still yield robust pretraining performance for LLMs. We hypothesize that essential linguistic patterns and semantic structures in high-quality source data can be preserved despite translation imperfectionsan attribute that could be particularly beneficial for cold-start pretraining in low-resource languages. If successful, this method could greatly expand access to multilingual pretraining data.
Specifically, we begin by segmenting English documents into sentences with the NLTK sentence tokenizer (Bird et al., 2009) and then translate them into target languages using the NLLB-200 model (Costa-jussà et al., 2022). The translated sentences are reassembled into documents, with their original order and structural elements intact (e.g., newline characters)3. We employ NLLB-200-1.3B, the smallest non-distilled model in the NLLB-200 suite, trying to explore the lower bound of translation quality. While this model may not represent the absolute lower bound, it provides a practical reference point for assessing the impact of noisier translations on LLM pretraining.
